  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...While oceanside impacts along the Outer Banks\nhave lessened, minor soundside flooding continues. CF Advisory\ncontinues through this morning's high tide, though the main \nthreat will be through the overnight before winds start to\nreally ease once shortwave pushes offshore. Long period swell \ncontinues to run higher than available wave guidance (Diamond\nstill reporting 11ft) and have had to manually up waves in \ncertain zones. This long period swell may still cause overwash\nissues at compromised dune locations.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drying, cool, and relatively \"calmer\" \nconditions expected across ENC through the first half of the \nweekend ahead of the next front to cross the area late SUN \nnight/early MON morning with a secondary reinforcing front to \nfollow shortly thereafter. This poses the next best chance for \nrain with NBM continuing to advertise PoPs>90%, but forecast has\ntrended ever slightly slower with the fronts' progression. QPF\nremains relatively low, especially compared to WED/THU's storm,\nbut the slower progression of the system may allow for some\nslightly higher amounts as represented in the slight uptick in\ncurrent forecast; highest rainfall amounts through FROPA AoB a \nquarter an inch. All liquid precip expected with temps in the \nlow 60s ahead of the front and upper 40s behind it. Prefrontal \nwinds may flirt with wind advisory criteria for portions of the \nimmediate coast (best chance E Carteret northward to Cape\nHatteras though areas along the coast from N Topsail through\nall of Hatteras Island could see Wind Advy levels). Calmer, \ncooler, and clearer behind these fronts through midweek with \nMaxTs ~60 and MinT ~freezing inland, mid- upper 40s/low 50s \ncoast through midweek.",
